      {
        "heading": "How to verify and close",
        "paragraphs": [
          "A resolution note should be short enough to read and specific enough to confirm. The analyst resolutions the preparation; the owner resolutions the decision. The best resolution is the one the next reviewer can confirm in under a minute. A measured resolution rate without a definition is just a number with confidence. For this step, the written action is to route any material gap to the finance owner.",
          "The cash forecast review should look at the oldest resolution, not only the newest. A account that needed a correction is useful proof about the routine. The forecast line entry should distinguish a confirmed resolution from an assumed one.",
          "For controllers, finance leaders, and business owners, a defensible resolution is worth more than a fast one. Keep the resolution rule fixed for the review window so the comparison means something. Close the forecast line only after the destination entry shows the approved change."
        ]
      }
